===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} to [[switch]] (each of two things)
#: {{coi|en|to '''interchange''' places}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} to [[mutually]] [[give]] and [[receive]] (something); to [[exchange]]
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 6-3|IV|vii|passage=I shall '''interchange''' / My waned state for Henry's regal crown.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} to [[swap]] or [[change places]]
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1996|author=Macy Nulman|title=The Encyclopedia of Jewish Prayer|page=260|text=The poem is written in an ''alef-lamed, bet-nun'' acrostic form. This is a formula of permutation of letters wherein the first '''interchanges''' with the twelfth, the second with the thirteenth, etc.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} to [[alternate]]; to [[intermingle]] or [[vary]]
#: ''to '''interchange''' cares with pleasures''
# {{lb|en|transport}} To act as or carry out an interchange (noun, senses 2, 3).
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2012|author=Andrew Martin|title=Underground Overground: A passenger's history of the Tube| publisher=Profile Books, {{ISBN|978-1846684777}}| page=274| text=But those 13 miles go through central London, and where the [[Underground]] '''interchanges''' with Crossrail, its own stations will be boosted and glamorised, especially at Bond Street and Tottenham Court Road.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2020 March 25|author=William Barter|title=HS2: Euston or Old Oak Common?|journal=Rail|page=59|text=The expectation is that about one-third of passengers will choose to '''interchange''' at Old Oak, while two-thirds will prefer to travel on to Euston.}}

